[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a minimal, copy-and-paste-ready example that shows how to:

1. **Add a raster-DEM source** (the source that actually contains the elevation data).  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that draws the shaded relief from that DEM.  

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from a raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.13.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.13.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like – here a simple OSM base)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base raster tiles (optional – you can use any vector style you prefer)
        'osm': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: [
            'https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://b.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://c.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'osm-basemap',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5], // Berlin
    zoom: 10,
    pitch: 60,           // tilt the map so the hillshade is visible
    bearing: -20
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the raster-DEM source.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // The DEM tiles must be in **Mapbox Terrain-RGB** format (or any
  // format that MapLibre can decode as a DEM).  For a quick demo you can
  // use the public demo tiles that MapLibre hosts.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      // URL template – you can replace this with your own DEM server.
      // The demo tiles are served with CORS enabled.
      tiles: [
        'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,
      // Optional: improve performance by limiting the maxzoom you need.
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the hillshade layer.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      // No source-layer needed for raster-dem sources.
      paint: {
        // These are the default values – tweak them to taste.
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 335, // degrees clockwise from north
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,           // 0 = flat, 1 = full relief
        'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
        'hillshade-accent-color': '#ffffff'
      }
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  (Optional) Add a terrain source so the map can be pitched
    //     and the camera can “fly over” the DEM.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What it adds | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Initialize the map** | A basic raster OSM basemap (or any style you prefer). | Gives you something to see under the hillshade. |
| **`raster-dem` source** | `type: "raster-dem"` with a `tiles` URL template. | This is the only source type that the `hillshade` layer can read. The tiles must contain elevation data (Mapbox Terrain-RGB is the most common format). |
| **`hillshade` layer** | `type: "hillshade"` referencing the DEM source. | Renders the shaded relief. The `paint` properties let you control direction, exaggeration, and colors. |
| **`setTerrain` (optional)** | Enables true 3-D terrain for the camera (pitch, fly-to, etc.). | Makes the hillshade look more natural when the map is tilted. Not required for the hillshade itself, but often used together. |

### Common gotchas & how to avoid them

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|----------|-----|
| **CORS errors** when using your own DEM tiles | Console shows “Cross-origin request blocked”. | Serve the DEM tiles with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). The demo tiles already have CORS enabled. |
| **Wrong tile format** (e.g., plain grayscale DEM) | Hillshade appears completely flat or missing. | Ensure the tiles are in **Terrain-RGB** (24-bit PNG) or another format MapLibre can decode as a DEM. |
| **Missing `maxzoom`** on the source | Very high-resolution tiles are requested unnecessarily, slowing the map. | Set `maxzoom` to the highest zoom level your DEM tiles actually provide. |
| **Hillshade not visible** | You see the basemap but no shading. | Make sure the map is pitched (`pitch > 0`) or the terrain is set (`map.setTerrain`). Hillshade is a relief effect that needs a non-zero pitch to be noticeable. |
| **Wrong `hillshade-illumination-direction`** | Shadows appear on the “wrong” side of the terrain. | Adjust the direction (0-360°) to match the sun position you want. |
| **Using a vector source** | `type: "hillshade"` refuses to load. | Hillshade only works with `raster-dem` sources; you cannot use a vector source for elevation. |

### Quick checklist before you go live

1. **DEM source** – `type: "raster-dem"` and a CORS-enabled tile URL.  
2. **Layer order** – Add the hillshade **after** the basemap but **before** any label or symbol layers you want to stay on top.  
3. **Pitch** – Set `pitch` > 0 (or call `map.setTerrain`) so the shading is visible.  
4. **Performance** – If you only need hillshade at low zooms, set `maxzoom` on the DEM source accordingly.  
5. **Styling** – Tweak the `paint` properties to match your visual design (exaggeration, colors, illumination direction).  

That’s all you need to get a hillshade layer working in MapLibre GL JS! Feel free to replace the demo DEM URL with your own tiles, adjust the paint values, and combine the layer with any other style you like. Happy mapping!
